menu "Radio Interface Configuration"

    config RADIO_BT_NAME
        string "Bluetooth device name"
        default "ESP32 Radio HF"
        help
            Name announced over Bluetooth (GAP local name) and used as the
            HFP Hands-Free service name in the SDP record.

    config RADIO_AG_ADDR
        string "Audio Gateway address for the test console"
        default "22:22:4F:BD:72:A0"
        help
            Bluetooth address (AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF) of the phone or PC that the
            serial test console connects to with the 'a' key. Only used for
            outgoing connections started from the console; incoming
            connections from any device are accepted regardless.

    config RADIO_SPP_CHANNEL
        int "RFCOMM channel of the SPP control service"
        range 2 30
        default 2
        help
            Channel 1 is used by HFP.

    config RADIO_SPP_SERVICE_NAME
        string "SPP service name"
        default "CAT Radio"

    choice RADIO_UART0_MODE
        prompt "UART0 (USB) mode"
        default RADIO_UART0_CONSOLE
        help
            UART0 is the USB serial port of the board (CH340G). It can be
            either the debug console or a wired control link that speaks the
            same protocol as the Bluetooth SPP service. Both cannot coexist
            on the same UART.

        config RADIO_UART0_CONSOLE
            bool "Console: logs, printf and the BTstack test console"
        config RADIO_UART0_CAT
            bool "CAT: wired host link with the control protocol (console muted)"
    endchoice

    config RADIO_HOST_SERIAL_BAUD
        int "Baud rate of the wired host link"
        depends on RADIO_UART0_CAT
        default 115200

    config RADIO_AUDIO_CAPTURE_ONLY
        bool "Diagnostic: capture-only audio (works around the swapped I2S data lines)"
        default n
        help
            TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC OPTION, for the bluesp32 board only.

            On that board the two I2S data nets reach the codec swapped: the
            codec's DACDAT (an input) hangs off the net that goes to IO35,
            which is input-only on the ESP32 and can never drive it, while the
            codec's ADCDAT (an output) hangs off the net that goes to IO26,
            which the driver uses as its output. The result is a constant tone
            instead of playback, and two drivers fighting over IO26.

            With this option enabled the I2S data input is moved to IO26, so
            the ESP32 reads the codec's real ADC output, and the data output is
            moved to IO23, which is unconnected on this board. That removes the
            contention and makes the capture direction (microphone towards the
            PC) work, so the diagnosis can be confirmed without reworking the
            board. Playback stays broken: that half cannot be fixed in
            software.

            Leave this off for a board whose R12/R14 wiring has been corrected.

endmenu
